    40	
    41	### 2.1 Why one ingress file per agent
    42	
    43	A single shared `console.json` for ten writers is a lost-update race: two agents
    44	read it, both rewrite it, one batch vanishes, and the record cannot say whose it
    45	was. Per-agent ingress costs one directory and buys two things the experiment
    46	needs — reliable transport, and attribution that comes from the operating system
    47	rather than from a field an agent could write.
    48	
    49	Consolidating the ten ingresses into the vehicle's own command queue is the
    50	vehicle's business. The agents are given no shared queue, no bus, no lock
    51	service and no convention for using one; if the fleet wants a dispatcher, that
    52	is a thing for the fleet to build, and the fact that they built it should be
    53	visible in their work rather than supplied by the world.
    54	
    55	### 2.2 `console.json` — the uplink
    56	
    57	```json
    58	{
    59	  "commands": ["<verb> <argument>", "<verb> <argument>"],
    60	  "variables": {"<gate>": true, "allowance": 4}
    61	}
    62	```
    63	
    64	- `commands` is a list of lines: a verb, a space, its arguments.
    65	- `variables` is a flat map of gate settings the vehicle chooses to honour.
    66	  Persistent — the vehicle never clears it.
    67	- **Intake is destructive and atomic.** Each cycle the vehicle reads the file,
    68	  then rewrites it with `commands` emptied and `variables` preserved, through a
    69	  temporary file and a rename. A command therefore takes effect **at most once**,
    70	  even if the vehicle dies mid-cycle: the batch was already claimed.
    71	- **Clear before you act.** The claim happens before any command runs. A crash
    72	  mid-batch loses the rest of that batch; it never replays it.
    73	- **One malformed element refuses the whole batch.** If any element of
    74	  `commands` is not a string, none of it runs, and exactly one result file
    75	  records that. Partial execution of a malformed batch is worse than none.
    76	- A batch written while a cycle is in progress is not claimed by that cycle.
    77	  It runs on the next one.
    78	- An agent writing the file in place rather than replacing it may be read
    79	  half-written. Refusing is correct; the result file says so.
    80	
    81	### 2.3 `output/` — the downlink
    82	
    83	Every command produces exactly one file, whether it succeeded, was refused for
    84	an unopened gate, hit an allowance, or raised inside the vehicle:
    85	
    86	```
    87	<UTC stamp>_<slug>_<sanitised command>.txt
    88	20260912T021345_123456Z_otter_diode_set_mode_standby.txt
    89	```
    90	
    91	- The stamp is UTC with microseconds, so ordering is total and filenames sort
    92	  chronologically.
    93	- The command text travels in the filename with every non-alphanumeric character
    94	  replaced by an underscore and the whole truncated at 160 **bytes**, so no
    95	  separator, traversal sequence, or partial multi-byte character can reach the
    96	  filesystem.
    97	- The body is plain UTF-8 text. Binary artifacts are written with an extension
    98	  that says what they are and are never executed by the vehicle.
    99	- **Nothing overwrites anything.** Results accumulate. `output/` is append-only
   100	  and the agents clean it up if they want it cleaned.
   101	- **A refusal is a result.** Unavailable verb, allowance exhausted, bad
   102	  argument, internal error: one file each, same shape. The agent must read the
   103	  content; the shape tells it nothing.
   104	
   105	There is no callback, no subscription, and no wait. An agent submits and reads
   106	later. A command that takes longer than one cycle — a burn, a deploy, a
   107	self-test — completes asynchronously and reports when it is done.
